result_text:
I reviewed the diff, which touches only `tests/Validation/ValidationEmailRuleTest.php`. I couldn't run the suite because this checkout has no `vendor/` directory and no `php` binary. So the findings about how egulias or PHP's built-in email filter treat specific addresses are based on reading the code, not on test runs. No CLAUDE.md rules apply to this file: there is no repo-level CLAUDE.md, and the rules in the user-level one don't cover code.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "tests/Validation/ValidationEmailRuleTest.php",
    "line": 331,
    "summary": "The literal '\"escaped\\\\\\\"quote\"@example.com' becomes the string \"escaped\\\\\"quote\" in PHP (an escaped backslash followed by a bare quote that closes the quoted string), not an escaped quote as the comment says; the test then asserts this broken address passes rfcCompliant().",
    "failure_scenario": "Single quotes turn \\\\\\\" into \\\\\". The quoted part ends after 'escaped\\\\', then 'quote\"@example.com' follows with a stray unclosed quote. If egulias RFCValidation rejects this (unclosed quoted string or ATEXT expected after the quote), the passes() assertion fails. Lines 543 and 703 repeat the same mislabeled pattern, so the 'escaped quote' case is never actually tested."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/Validation/ValidationEmailRuleTest.php",
    "line": 612,
    "summary": "' space@domain.com' is asserted to fail rfcCompliant(), but egulias RFCValidation treats leading whitespace as folding whitespace and may only raise a warning rather than reject the address.",
    "failure_scenario": "RFCValidation (non-strict) ignores warnings, so the address can come back valid. The fails() assertion for rfcCompliant() then breaks testNativeValidationVsRfcCompliant. Only strict mode (NoRFCWarningsValidation) would reliably reject it."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/Validation/ValidationEmailRuleTest.php",
