    "summary": "'user@[IPv6:::]' is commented as 'invalid shorthand IPv6' but is asserted to pass rfcCompliant(), so the test locks in acceptance of something its own author calls invalid.",
    "failure_scenario": "Either the comment is wrong ('::' is the valid unspecified address) or the expectation is wrong. If a later egulias release tightens IPv6 literal checks, the test fails, and anyone reading it can't tell which behavior is intended."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/Validation/ValidationEmailRuleTest.php",
    "line": 325,
    "summary": "The new tests pin many edge-case results (IPv6/IPv4 literals, Unicode/IDN domains, comments, quoted local parts) that depend on the egulias version, but composer.json allows egulias/email-validator ^3.2.1|^4.0 and FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL changes between PHP versions.",
    "failure_scenario": "A prefer-lowest CI run on egulias 3.x, or a different PHP minor version, can classify cases like 'user@bücher.example', 'пример@пример.рф' (strict) or 'user@[IPv6:::1]' differently. The test then fails because a library version changed, not because Laravel did."
